Given, \[V_{\text{rms}}=4\ \text{V}\] \[f_m=1\ \text{kHz}\] \[K_p=2\ \text{rad/V}\] \[f_c=10^6\ \text{Hz}\]The peak value of the message signal is \[V_m=\sqrt{2}\,V_{\text{rms}}\] \[V_m=4\sqrt{2}\ \text{V}\]Hence, \[m(t)=4\sqrt{2}\sin(2\pi\times10^3 t).\]For phase modulation, \[s(t)=A_c\cos\!\left(2\pi f_c t+K_p m(t)\right).\]The instantaneous frequency is \[f_i(t)
=
f_c+\frac{K_p}{2\pi}\frac{dm(t)}{dt}.\]Differentiating \(m(t)\), \[\frac{dm(t)}{dt}
=
4\sqrt{2}\,(2\pi\times10^3)\cos(2\pi\times10^3 t).\]The maximum value occurs when \[\cos(2\pi\times10^3 t)=1.\]Therefore, \[\left(\frac{dm(t)}{dt}\right)_{\max}
=
4\sqrt{2}\,(2\pi\times10^3).\]Substituting into the instantaneous frequency expression, \[(f_i)_{\max}
=
10^6
+
\frac{2}{2\pi}
\left[
4\sqrt{2}\,(2\pi\times10^3)
\right].\] \[(f_i)_{\max}
=
10^6+8000\sqrt{2}.\] \[(f_i)_{\max}
=
10^6+11313.7085.\] \[(f_i)_{\max}
=
1011313.7085\ \text{Hz}.\]Hence, \[\boxed{(f_i)_{\max}=1011313.7\ \text{Hz}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>For PM, \[f_i(t)=f_c+\frac{K_p}{2\pi}\frac{dm(t)}{dt},\] so frequency deviation depends on the derivative of the message signal.</li><li>Do not use \[\Delta f=K_p m(t),\] which corresponds to phase deviation, not frequency deviation.</li><li>Convert RMS value to peak value correctly: \[V_m=\sqrt{2}\,V_{\text{rms}}.\]</li><li>The carrier amplitude \(A_c=2\) has no effect on the instantaneous frequency.</li></ul></div>

Given,\[\mu = 0.5\]For a conventional AM signal, \[P_t = P_c\left(1+\frac{\mu^2}{2}\right)\]Substituting \(\mu=0.5\), \[P_t
=
P_c\left(1+\frac{(0.5)^2}{2}\right)
=
P_c(1.125)\]The power in each sideband is \[P_{SB}
=
\frac{P_c\mu^2}{4}\]When the carrier and one sideband are suppressed, the power saved is \[P_{\text{saved}}
=
P_c+\frac{P_c\mu^2}{4}.\]Therefore, \[\frac{P_{\text{saved}}}{P_t}
=
\frac{P_c+\dfrac{P_c\mu^2}{4}}
     {P_c\left(1+\dfrac{\mu^2}{2}\right)}
=
\frac{1+\dfrac{\mu^2}{4}}
     {1+\dfrac{\mu^2}{2}}.\]Substituting \(\mu=0.5\), \[\frac{P_{\text{saved}}}{P_t}
=
\frac{1+\dfrac{0.25}{4}}
     {1+\dfrac{0.25}{2}}
=
\frac{1.0625}{1.125}
=
0.9444.\]Hence, \[\%\,\text{Power Saved}
=
0.9444\times100
=
94.44\%.\] \[\boxed{\%\,\text{Power Saved}=94.4\%}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>For AM, \[P_t=P_c\left(1+\frac{\mu^2}{2}\right),\] not \(P_t=P_c(1+\mu^2)\).</li><li>Each sideband contains \[\frac{P_c\mu^2}{4}\] power.</li><li>If the carrier and one sideband are removed, the remaining signal is SSB-SC.</li></ul></div>
